      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The role of coronary atherosclerosis (CA+) in ventricular remodeling after aortic valve replacement (AVR) for isolated aortic stenosis (AS) is not well defined. We sought to evaluate the impact of not revascularized moderate coronary atherosclerosis in long-term left ventricular (LV) remodeling after AVR."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We assessed by coronariography the coronary artery disease in 66 patients referred for AVR and evaluated morphological and functional LV data by echocardiography both preoperatively and postoperatively (3 ± 1.2 years)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In patients without coronary atherosclerosis, hypertrophy regression was more intense and the absolute reverse remodeling was higher in LV mass index (-55.8 ± 36 g/m² vs -28.4 ± 34 g/m², p = 0.004), reduction of LV dimensions (LV end-diastolic diameter [LVEDD]: -4.1 ± 7.4 mm vs -2.2 ± 8.3 mm, p = 0.04), and regression of wall thickness (interventricular septum [IVS]: -3.3 ± 2.6 mm vs -1.6 ± 2.2 mm, p = 0.01; and posterior wall thickness [PWT]: -2.1 ± 2.1 mm vs 0.6 ± 2.1 mm, p = 0.012)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "After AVR for AS, not revascularized moderate coronary atherosclerosis determines a long-term lesser degree of LV hypertrophy regression and a worse absolute reverse remodeling of LV mass index, LVEDD, IVS and PWT."
        }
      ]},
